#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:7
#: 7e94d07ad1b54336a29fc5ff9a5fbfa3
msgid "In-Flight FFT-Based Harmonic Notch Setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:9
#: d5f9b20b93504e1da40248b066ba0a2c
msgid "This feature is included in autopilots with 2MB of memory. Check your autopilot's :ref:`binary-features` to determine if your autopilot has this feature (GyroFFT). Also, only 1 FFT-based Notch can be set up."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:13
#: 7d7d9d849d5c461aba1b5837eacd6c05
msgid "ArduPilot comes pre-configured with appropriate defaults for all FFT settings. The only initial setup required is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:15
#: 6702e0280dd24139beb4e8914e81734b
msgid "Set :ref:`FFT_ENABLE <FFT_ENABLE>` = 1 to enable the FFT engine. This then requires that you reboot your autopilot after which FFT support will be enabled and other FFT parameters should be visible in your GCS. With default parameter settings the FFT engine will run a self-check for frequency matching on your hardware. If you do not see any FFT errors then things are working properly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:17
#: 6a6189b746d84743a512d627285551b3
msgid "With FFT enabled it is best to first perform a test flight to check that your aircraft's particular noise frequencies are being captured and to monitor CPU load. See :ref:`Initial Analysis Flight <common-imu-fft-test-flight>`. Normally, results from this will show clear noise recognition and acceptable cpu loading, and  then you can use the FFT to drive the :ref:`harmonic notch<common-imu-notch-filtering>` by setting these parameters:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:19
#: 9570e9be6f734c61893b702647ec50d2
msgid "Set :ref:`INS_HNTCH_ENABLE <INS_HNTCH_ENABLE>` and/or :ref:`INS_HNTC2_ENABLE <INS_HNTC2_ENABLE>` = 1 to enable the harmonic notch = 1 to enable the harmonic notch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:20
#: 8b152e3690f2418a8b06d4c1d4261df9
msgid "Set :ref:`INS_HNTCH_MODE <INS_HNTCH_MODE>` and/or :ref:`INS_HNTC2_MODE <INS_HNTC2_MODE>` = 4 to use the FFT detected frequency for controlling the harmonic notch frequency."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:21
#: 0707b3a5692b4dd2b64e41de576e3f83
msgid "Set :ref:`INS_HNTCH_REF <INS_HNTCH_REF>` and/or :ref:`INS_HNTC2_REF <INS_HNTC2_REF>` = 1 to set the harmonic notch reference value, which for FFT analysis generally means no scaling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:22
#: 1473189964c3475791b7545cbfdb9b66
msgid "The harmonic notch reference frequency parameter, :ref:`INS_HNTCH_FREQ <INS_HNTCH_FREQ>` and/or :ref:`INS_HNTC2_FREQ <INS_HNTC2_FREQ>`, is used to indicate the lowest motor speed for which the ESC telemetry should be used to dynamically set the harmonic notch reference frequency."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:24
#: 570021d5d4954d5c82df09bd43c92706
msgid "For most uses with other FFT-related advanced parameters at their default, this is all that is required. The user can do optimization of the filtering setup by analyzing the test flight logs and adjusting notch bandwidth, if desired, by following the :ref:`In-flight FFT Advanced Setup <common-imu-fft-advanced-setup>` instructions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:26
#: a39a8e0150934d4db86e6c1081b10141
msgid "Setting up the FFT parameters can be done automatically using the ``RCx_OPTION`` auxiliary function \"162\" on a transmitter switch. Set the function to a switch on the transmitter. Hover the vehicle, switch it on (high) for 30 seconds, and switch back low and land. The parameters will have been set up and the switch function removed. NOTE: do not use this feature in firmware version 4.3!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:28
#: ddf21792f3c8410da2280815c2c55883
msgid "Using In-Flight FFT can result in poorer performance than a properly set up :ref:`Throttle-Based <common-throttle-based-notch>` notch filter since the FFT computations take time and can lag the actual required center frequency. In-Flight FFT is useful when the rotor frequencies of the vehicle vary widely as in heavy lift vehicles operating with high and low loads. It can be useful in setting up :ref:`Throttle-Based <common-throttle-based-notch>` notch filters, however, see :ref:`In-flight FFT Advanced Setup <common-imu-fft-advanced-setup>` instructions for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:31
#: 6508a4f1d51a42d89c808684e0ae45e5
msgid "FFT Dynamic Harmonic Notch Frequency Tracking"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:33
#: 43dbad4106ca4a8fbc9ad62d006dbb99
msgid "FFT mode tracking sets the base frequency to the largest noise peak. Normally, when multiple harmonic notch filters are then enabled, the center frequency of each harmonic is locked to the base frequency of the first filter as an integer multiple, as determined by :ref:`INS_HNTCH_HMNCS <INS_HNTCH_HMNCS>`. The bandwidth of the notch (and any enabled harmonic notches) will maintain a ratio set by the _FREQ/_BW params as the center frequency moves. Setting bit 1 of :ref:`INS_HNTCH_OPTS<INS_HNTCH_OPTS>`, or :ref:`INS_HNTC2_OPTS<INS_HNTC2_OPTS>`, will enable each harmonic filter to track the three largest noise peaks, individually."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:35
#: 33ba300edea5480cb202ff5f80924210
msgid "setting bit 1 of the notch options will also change the default value of :ref:`INS_HNTCH_HMNCS <INS_HNTCH_HMNCS>` to 1 instead of its normal 3. This is to maintain backwards compatibility with previous firmware versions. You can set :ref:`INS_HNTCH_HMNCS <INS_HNTCH_HMNCS>` back to 3, or whatever is desired, after setting bit 1 of :ref:`INS_HNTCH_HMNCS <INS_HNTCH_HMNCS>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:37
#: ca199d21089845a39435ab7b6f446da4
msgid "If you set bit 1 of :ref:`INS_HNTCH_OPTS<INS_HNTCH_OPTS>`, or :ref:`INS_HNTC2_OPTS<INS_HNTC2_OPTS>`, the bandwidth should not be half the frequency(default). It should be greatly reduced as more notches (3x more since now tracking three noise peaks) cause more phase lag (i.e. latency), thus the bandwidth needs to be reduced to maintain a reasonable phase lag, else oscillation and a poorer tune will result despite the more accurate filtering compared to throttle-based filtering. The suggested starting point is setting the _FREQ/_BW params to 4/1 ratio instead of the default 2/1. This is because for notch filters, a wider bandwidth causes a greater phase lag per notch. You can also use the `Filter Tool <https://firmware.ardupilot.org/Tools/FilterTool/>`__ to check the phase lag for your chosen filtering settings. If your phase lag is higher than it was with the single peak filtering, then you can try reducing the bandwidth even further, balancing the phase lag and the amount of noise in the system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:43
#: ffb8a259138044e88429a76169131ebc
msgid "FFT Options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:45
#: f154c9d699cd45d693e41e2ecd5e30bf
msgid "There are two options that can be selected by setting the appropriate bit in the :ref:`FFT_OPTIONS<FFT_OPTIONS>` parameter that affect FFT operation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:48
#: fcc6a7e4c02045f1a93a70da4890a95f
msgid "Post Filter Chain FFT Analysis Window"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:50
#: b1cee7103ecb45fca7bc4eca59f7796f
msgid "Normally, the FFT analysis for adjusting the center frequency is done by measuring the noise directly at the output of the unfiltered gyro data. However, if bit 0 of :ref:`FFT_OPTIONS<FFT_OPTIONS>` is set, then the measurement window takes into account the effects of the low pass filter and any configured notch filter(s). This is useful if there is high-frequency noise, which impacts the control response less than lower-frequency noise due to the low pass at the end of the filter chain but may be targeted by the FFT measurement. Setting this bit will only track those lower frequency, and more critical, noise peaks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:53
#: 6dd5b34228484b5999f2fe1c0d2401f5
msgid "Motor Noise Check"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:55
#: 264d43a5740a46b8907dc39bb5b88423
msgid "If bit 1 of :ref:`FFT_OPTIONS<FFT_OPTIONS>` is set and ESC motor rpm telemetry is available, then the measurement window for the FFT is centered about the motor(s) frequency as reported by ESC telemetry. This will generate a GCS warning message if noise from any motor greater than 40db is passing through the filter chain, and identify its level, motor number, and frequency. This bit must be used with bit 0 also set."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:58
#: 149349b740614ac7930f504991be0ea9
msgid "Typical Use"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-imu-fft.rst:60
#: ecf0e14a22e6461ca7a1dbbc6b159a1b
msgid "A typical use of the FFT notch filter is in addition to other dynamic harmonic notch filters (Throttle, ESC, or RPM-based). In these configurations, using the post-LPF FFT Window :ref:`FFT_OPTIONS<FFT_OPTIONS>` bit will yield the best overall filtering results by positioning the FFT filter to target noise not filtered by the other notch filter and gyro LPF (:ref:`INS_GYRO_FILTER<INS_GYRO_FILTER>`."
msgstr ""

